  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss. The left-right direction is steeper, so it overshoots first. Plain gradient descent there diverges above a learning rate of 2/2.5 = 0.8.",
    "Noise is Gaussian and centred on the true slope. Its spread is 1.5·√((64−B)/(63·B)) for batch size B, so it is exactly 0 at B = 64. The same seeded sequence replays after Reset, and New run uses the next values.",
    "Each arrow is drawn 2× the length of its step: the orange arrow is the actual mini-batch step, the grey one is learning rate × the true downhill slope. The step-length readout gives the true length.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished). It is marked diverged when the loss goes above 8 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. Then the ball is held at the bowl's edge, and the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Play advances 5 steps per second at speed 1. The trail holds the start point plus up to 40 landing points, and so does the ghost."
  ]
}